Here's an updated version of the `longestCommonSubsequence` class that prints the LCSubsequence:

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] memo;
    public String longestCommonSubsequence(String text1, String text2) {
        int n = text1.length();
        int m = text2.length();
        memo = new int[n][m];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            Arrays.fill(memo[i], -1);
        }
        return dfs(0, 0, text1, text2);
    }
    public String dfs(int i, int j, String t1, String t2) {
        if (i == t1.length() || j == t2.length()) return "";
        if (memo[i][j]!= -1) return memo[i][j] == 0? "" : memo[i][j] + "";
        String res = "";
        if (t1.charAt(i) == t2.charAt(j)) {
            res = String.valueOf(t1.charAt(i)) + dfs(i+1, j+1, t1, t2);
        } else {
            String s1 = dfs(i+1, j, t1, t2);
            String s2 = dfs(i, j+1, t1, t2);
            res = s1.length() > s2.length()? s1 : s2;
        }
        memo[i][j] = res.length();
        return res;
    }
}
```

This code returns the LCSubsequence as a string.

