System.Text.Json is a modern JSON serializer and deserializer developed by Microsoft, which provides a fast and efficient way to work with JSON data in.NET applications.

To deserialize a list of objects using System.Text.Json, you can follow these general steps:

1. Create a class that represents the type of object you want to deserialize:

```
public class MyObject
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    // Add other properties as needed
}
```

2. Create a list of instances of this class:

```
List<MyObject> myObjects = new List<MyObject>
{
    new MyObject { Id = 1, Name = "Object 1" },
    new MyObject { Id = 2, Name = "Object 2" },
    // Add more objects as needed
};
```

3. Use the `JsonSerializer` class from System.Text.Json to serialize this list to a JSON string:

```
string jsonString = JsonSerializer.Serialize(myObjects);
```

4. To deserialize the JSON string back into a list of objects, you can use the `JsonSerializer` class again:

```
List<MyObject> deserializedObjects = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<List<MyObject>>(jsonString);
```

This will create a new list of `MyObject` instances that has the same content as the original list.

Here's the complete code:

```
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text.Json;

public class MyObject
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        List<MyObject> myObjects = new List<MyObject>
        {
            new MyObject { Id = 1, Name = "Object 1" },
            new MyObject { Id = 2, Name = "Object 2" },
        };

        string jsonString = JsonSerializer.Serialize(myObjects);

        List<MyObject> deserializedObjects = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<List<MyObject>>(jsonString);

        foreach (MyObject obj in deserializedObjects)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Id: {obj.Id}, Name: {obj.Name}");
        }
    }
}
```

This code will output:

```
Id: 1, Name: Object 1
Id: 2, Name: Object 2
```

