Since you're using Dapper as your ORM, you're probably already familiar with its syntax and ways of interacting with your MySQL database. Uploading files to the database is generally not recommended as it can lead to performance issues and security concerns. Instead, it's better to store the file on your server or in a cloud storage solution, and store the file path or URL in your database.

Assuming you still want to store the file in your database, you can create a controller that accepts a file upload and leverages Dapper to store the file data in the database.

Here's an example of how you can create a controller in C# (using.NET Core) to handle file uploads and store the file data in your MySQL database using Dapper:
```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Dapper;
using MySql.Data.MySqlClient;
using System.IO;

namespace YourNamespace.Controllers
{
    [ApiController]
    [Route("api/[controller]")]
    public class FileUploadController : ControllerBase
    {
        private readonly string _connectionString;

        public FileUploadController(string connectionString)
        {
            _connectionString = connectionString;
        }

        [HttpPost]
        public IActionResult UploadFile(IFormFile file)
        {
            if (file == null || file.Length == 0)
                return BadRequest("No file was provided");

            using (var connection = new MySqlConnection(_connectionString))
            {
                connection.Open();
                var query = @"INSERT INTO your_table (file_data) VALUES (@fileData)";
                var command = new MySqlCommand(query, connection);
                command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@fileData", file.OpenReadStream());

                command.ExecuteNonQuery();
            }

            return Ok("File uploaded successfully");
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `FileUploadController` has a single endpoint (`UploadFile`) that accepts an `IFormFile` object (which represents the file uploaded by the client).

The controller checks if a file was provided, and if so, it creates a connection to your MySQL database using Dapper. It then executes an INSERT query to store the file data in your database table. In this example, `your_table` is the name of the table where you want to store the file data, and `@fileData` is the column where you want to store the file data.

Note that you'll need to replace `your_table` and `@fileData` with the actual name of your table and column.

